# Cross-origin isolation, for every document this site serves.
#
# The FSRS optimizer is a threaded wasm build, so it needs SharedArrayBuffer,
# which browsers only grant to a cross-origin isolated document. `require-corp`
# is the mode Safari supports.
#
# This file is now load-bearing rather than a belt to some braces. The app used
# to be rendered by a Cloudflare Worker whose route rules set these headers; it
# is a static bundle on Pages now, so this is the only place they come from. If
# it stops being read, auto-calibration silently declines to start and says so
# only in a console line (see src/zero/auto-calibration.ts).
#
# Worth checking after a deploy: `crossOriginIsolated` in the console must be
# true.
#
# The cost of `require-corp` is that cross-origin subresources without a CORP
# header stop loading, which is why the Google avatar goes through the API's
# /api/avatar rather than straight into an <img> — and why that endpoint answers
# `cross-origin` rather than `same-origin`, now that it is not our origin.
/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp

# Hashed filenames, so they can never go stale.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
